  abstract={Topology optimization of heatsink design is a prominent topic in power electronics today. To simplify the design process, this paper compares the accuracy of four different Artificial Intelligence (AI) models—Feedforward Neural Network (FNN), Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), Transfer Learning (TL), and Conditional Generative Adversarial Network (CGaNet)—in predicting heatsink temperatures and testing their extrapolation abilities. Initially, a cylindrical pin heatsink 3D model was built, and two datasets were collected from Ansys Icepak, considering five parameters. The models were trained using the first dataset, which included power ranges below 12.5W. Subsequently, the second dataset, with power ranges above 12.5W, was used to test the pre-trained models’ extrapolation abilities. The final results indicate that transfer learning, with the first three layers frozen, achieved the highest accuracy when compared to the other models using a limited new domain dataset. However, it is found that CGaNet shows good extrapolation performance when a new domain dataset is not available.},
